Universal 0.4.1: Read Me.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Tested on NetBSD 4.0 (i386), Debian 4.0 (i386, x86_64, ppc, ia64), 
Debian 5.0 (i386, x86_64, ppc, ia64), CentOS 4.8 (i386, x86_64) and 
CentOS 5.4 (i386, x86_64). Verified working for all services in the 
universal(-rhel4).role as of 18/01/2010. More platforms will follow.

Universal is the provisioning component of OpenPCF, the "Open (Source) 
Provisioning and Control Framework". It essentially allows you to automate
the customization of a freshly installed system which in turn allows for 
exact duplicates of a system with little to no effort, or a restore of a 
system after a crash, without the doubt that comes from the fact that all
customizations after installation were undocumented.

You can use Universal to set up a system for the first time in a situation 
when there are no (or at least very little) services available in your 
environment (for example, in a new datacenter). Universal then configures
the services that allow you to roll out multiple systems using daemons
like cfengine, yum (httpd), nfs and samba. 


Universal works using the following concepts:

    A machine is "set up" on a "platform" to play a particular "role".


A "setup" comprises the site-specific settings for the environment.
Think domain name, IP address ranges, and company name.  These are set
in a configuration file.

A "platform" is often just a particular operating system release; but more
abstractly speaking, it is a particular set of tool (versions) that Universal
uses that are available on the machine, along with the default locations of
various configuration specific files and directories.

A "role" consists of "services"; it is the software needed to perform some
useful function.  The "services" are defined by modules in the Services/
directory.  Note that not all roles are available on all platforms: for
example, RHEL4 compatible systems (CentOS 4 too) have dovecot 0.x and so
cannot fill roles requireing dovecot 1.x.


A quick overview of the chief Universal software components:

Configuration/:
Contains the site specific configuration files named either "setup.conf" or 
"$myhostname.conf", which is used by the various subsystems. In the future 
this directory might contain other configuration files. 

Includes/:
This contains the global functions and variables that are used in almost
all scripts and tools.

Platforms/:
Contains the definition files (platform descriptors) for a given "platform".
This ensures that the various services know where stuff should go. 
For example, on one system /etc/certs is the preferred location for x509
certificates, but on the other it could be /etc/ssl/certs!

Roles/: 
Contains the definitions for "roles" which are used in the site 
specific configuration to determine what needs to be installed and 
configured when a certain server role is requested.

Services/:
Contains the various installation subsystems that take care of running
the various scripts that modify and install services. A service is a very 
broad descriptor, like "dns". that holds a list of possible providers of 
that service, like bind9, tinydns, etc.

Tools/:
This contains various subsystems that handle various issues like certificate
generation, package creation/testing/installation, sanity checking and 
network configuration setup.


A really really really quick how-to:

1: Make sure you've freshly installed one of the supported platforms
   mentioned above and make sure you've connected to any required extra
   repositories:
    * For NetBSD 4:
       - Pkgsrc: http://www.pkgsrc.org/

    * For Debian 4:
       - Backports: http://backports.org/

    * For CentOS or RHEL 4:
       - EPEL 4: https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/EPEL/FAQ#howtouse
       - OPCF 4: http://www.Universal.org/downloads/repository/Universal/rhel/4.x/os/

    * For CentOS or RHEL 5:
       - EPEL 5: https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/EPEL/FAQ#howtouse
       - OPCF 5: http://www.Universal.org/downloads/repository/Universal/rhel/5.x/os/
   
   To connect to the Universal repository, visit one of the OPCF links above
   and select your platform (i386 or x86_64). Enter the RPMS subdirectory and
   download the "Universal-release" rpm (like Universal-release-?-?.noarch.rpm).
   Install it with "rpm -Uvh Universal-release*".

   If you're using real RHEL 4, make sure you've installed the yum package
   first! RHEL 4 default has no yum. These packages are provided by the
   OPCF 4 repository. For more info take a look at:

   * http://wiki.centos.org/HowTos/PackageManagement/YumOnRHEL

   For more detailed information about the supported platforms of Universal
   and specific instructions for setting up and connecting to external
   repositories take a look at our supported platforms page:

   * http://www.Universal.org/wiki/SupportedPlatforms

2: Turn off any firewall and RBAC-like software like SELinux or AppArmor.
   For platform specific instructions, take a look at our supported platforms
   page on Universal.org (see above).

3: Download or checkout Universal and place it somewhere on the target 
   machine, like /root/Universal

4: Go into /root/Universal/Configuration and copy example.conf to setup.conf.
   Edit setup.conf to match your needs. Note that multiple networks are
   supported but you need to make sure that the resulting listening IP
   actually is 'live' on the box ('fine-grained' IP sanity checking is in
   the works). Make sure you select the 'universal.role' to have all 
   services configured that Universal knows about (always a good test-case).
   Also make sure you specify a platform that corresponds with a file in
   the /root/Universal/Platforms directory.

5: Go into /root/Universal and run "./Universal.sh" without arguments. you should
   see a Usage message with quite a few options. This means your config is
   sane as far as Universal can tell.

6: Run "./Universal.sh deps | sort | uniq" and make 100% sure you install
   the package list that Universal tells you to! Hint for RHEL or CentOS based
   systems: "yum install `./Universal.sh deps | sort | uniq`". Hint for Debian
   based systems: "apt-get install `./Universal.sh deps | sort | uniq`"

7: Make sure you're not already running any of the services you want
   Universal to set-up for you. Debian has a tendency of automatically starting 
   services that you've just installed! Make sure they're off by running:

   /root/Universal/Universal.sh stop 

   This will attempt to stop all services that Universal wants to configure for
   you. Note that on Debian, the NFS init scripts are kind of strange. In our
   experience it takes at least 4! /etc/init.d/nfs stop commands or Universal.sh
   stop runs to actually make it STOP listening on port 2049.

8: Run "./Universal.sh build". This will result in the building of configuration
   for your system in the /root/Universal/Build directory. Go ahead and inspect
   it. If you like it, go ahead to the next step.

9: Run "./Universal.sh install". This will install the freshly build config into
   your system. Universal in general never deletes or replaces something without
   the possibility of undo, but if you really want to be sure, backup first.

10:Run "./Universal.sh start". This will enable the boot-time init.d or rc.conf
   scripts so your system comes up with these services starting in the correct
   order and directly starts the services for you.


Optional notes after the howto:

You can almost always undo an installation. Just run the following steps in
the specified order in /root/Universal:

1: "./Universal.sh stop"
2: "./Universal.sh uninstall"

You can now verify that the configuration is not on the system anymore and 
that the services have been stopped and removed from the boot-time config.
If you want to clean your /root/Universal/Build directory, run:

3: "./Universal.sh clean"

Your build directory should now be empty and you can start a-fresh if you're
so inclined.


Disclaimer just so you know:

Universal is software that changes the configuration of running systems. If you
run Universal without knowing what you're doing (on say, systems that are 
already live and in production), you can seriously hurt your carreer. Be sure
to know what you're doing (and if unsure, just don't do it!).

